{"abstract":"Context: Roads are ubiquitous in human inhabited landscapes, and can impact animal movement and population dynamics, due to barrier effects, road mortality, but also by providing resources at road verges. Thus, we need a better understanding of how roads, in interaction with seasonal changes in habitat structure, affect space use and habitat selection of the animals that persist in these landscapes. Objectives: Here, we used the European hare (Lepus europaeus) as model species to investigate how human-induced changes in landscape composition—measured as road density, land cover type, and field size—affect home range location, seasonal habitat selection and road crossings, which are likely to correlate with wildlife-vehicle collision risk. Methods: We collected > 240,000 GPS positions of 90 hares from three populations (one in Denmark and two in Germany) that differed regarding agricultural intensification and road density. Using this data, we analyzed home range location and habitat selection (using step-selection functions) in relation to roads, habitat composition, and seasonality, and quantified how these factors affected road crossings by hares. Results: In comparatively more heterogeneous landscapes, hares established home ranges in areas with lower road densities compared to the surrounding area, but not in more simple landscapes. Moreover, hares generally avoided main roads and selected for minor roads during the vegetation growth seasons, especially in areas with comparatively less heterogeneous habitat structure. Hares crossed more main roads when moving greater distances, with movement distances being comparatively larger in simpler landscapes. Conclusions: Our findings emphasize that it is important to distinguish between road types, as different roads can have different impacts on animals (e.g., small roads providing foraging opportunities via roadside vegetation and large roads being avoided). Moreover, animals in comparatively more heterogeneous landscapes are better able to adjust their habitat selection to avoid main roads than animals inhabiting simpler landscapes. More generally, homogenous landscapes increase the space use requirements of animals, leading to increased probability of road crossings, which in turn might affect population dynamics via increased road mortality risk.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Mayer, Martin","Fischer, Christina","Blaum, Niels","Sunde, Peter","Ullmann, Wiebke"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-211109"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Mayer, Martin"],"country":["Germany","Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Anhalt University of Applied Sciences","Leibniz Centre for Agricultural Landscape Research","University of Inland Norway","University of Potsdam"],"keyword":["Agricultural intensification","GPS","Habitat fragmentation","Habitat selection","Lepus europaeus","Road ecology"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"influence of roads on space use by european hares in different landscapes","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-022-01552-3","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-211109","identifier":[{"id":"85143846921","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-8f562ffd-5fd9-41a9-b5be-57fa4f7d398a"],"keyword":["Agricultural intensification","GPS","Habitat fragmentation","Habitat selection","Lepus europaeus","Road ecology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-85D80EBA4AAA73227D76D51B63862DD7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-022-01552-3","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/404888642/s10980-022-01552-3.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Ecoscience, Department of Ecoscience - Wildlife Ecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"no","key":"university of inland norway","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Inland Norway","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"anhalt university of applied sciences","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Anhalt University of Applied Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"university of potsdam","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Potsdam","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"de","key":"leibniz centre for agricultural landscape research","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Leibniz Centre for Agricultural Landscape Research","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Martin","key":"mayerpau","last":"Mayer","locid":"bfc4db27-d06d-4e2a-8e51-272b4dbf95de","name":"Mayer, Martin","orcid":"0000-0002-9905-3625","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Christina","key":"fischerpau","last":"Fischer","name":"Fischer, Christina","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Niels","key":"blaumpau","last":"Blaum","name":"Blaum, Niels","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"sundepau","last":"Sunde","locid":"3a9b01f6-a55e-40f0-9f76-9f8bb4d5d466","name":"Sunde, Peter","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Wiebke","key":"ullmannpau","last":"Ullmann","name":"Ullmann, Wiebke","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10980-022-01552-3","issn":["09212973","15729761"],"issn_org":["09212973"],"issue":"1","journal":"Landscape Ecology","pages":"131-146","publisher":"Springer Science and Business Media BV","publisher_org":"Springer Science and Business Media B.V.","status":"p","title":"Landscape Ecology","volume":"38"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85143846921"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-8f562ffd-5fd9-41a9-b5be-57fa4f7d398a"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Influence of roads on space use by European hares in different landscapes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-211109","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Non-communicable diseases (NCDs), including coronary heart disease, stroke, hypertension, type 2 diabetes, dementia, depression and cancers, are on the rise worldwide and are often associated with a lack of physical activity (PA). Globally, the levels of PA among individuals are below WHO recommendations. A lack of PA can increase morbidity and mortality, worsen the quality of life and increase the economic burden on individuals and society. In response to this trend, numerous organisations came together under one umbrella in Hamburg, Germany, in April 2021 and signed the 'Hamburg Declaration'. This represented an international commitment to take all necessary actions to increase PA and improve the health of individuals to entire communities. Individuals and organisations are working together as the 'Global Alliance for the Promotion of Physical Activity' to drive long-term individual and population-wide behaviour change by collaborating with all stakeholders in the community: active hospitals, physical activity specialists, community services and healthcare providers, all achieving sustainable health goals for their patients/clients. The 'Hamburg Declaration' calls on national and international policymakers to take concrete action to promote daily PA and exercise at a population level and in healthcare settings.","author_count":113,"contributors":["Steinacker, Jürgen M","van Mechelen, Willem","Bloch, Wilhelm","Börjesson, Mats","Casasco, Maurizio","Wolfarth, Bernd","Knoke, Carolin","Papadopoulou, Theodora","Wendt, Janine","Al Tunaiji, Hashel","Andresen, Dietrich","Andrieieva, Olena","Bachl, Norbert","Badtieva, Victoriya","Beucher, Friedhelm J","Blauwet, Cheri A","Casajus Mallen, Jose-Antonio","Chang, Ju-Ho","Clénin, German","Constantini, Naama","Constantinou, Demitri","Di Luigi, Luigi","Declercq, Lukas","Doutreleau, Stephane","Drozdovska, Svitlana","Duclos, Martine","Ermolao, Andrea","Fischbach, Thomas","Fischer, Anastasia N","Fossati, Chiara","Franchel