{"abstract":"Many experiments have shown that biodiversity enhances ecosystem functioning. However, we have little understanding of how environmental heterogeneity shapes the effect of diversity on ecosystem functioning and to what extent this diversity effect is mediated by variation in species richness or species turnover. This knowledge is crucial to scaling up the results of experiments from local to regional scales. Here we quantify the diversity effect and its components—that is, the contributions of variation in species richness and species turnover—for 22 ecosystem functions of microorganisms, plants and animals across 13 major ecosystem types on Mt Kilimanjaro, Tanzania. Environmental heterogeneity across ecosystem types on average increased the diversity effect from explaining 49% to 72% of the variation in ecosystem functions. In contrast to our expectation, the diversity effect was more strongly mediated by variation in species richness than by species turnover. Our findings reveal that environmental heterogeneity strengthens the relationship between biodiversity and ecosystem functioning and that species richness is a stronger driver of ecosystem functioning than species turnover. Based on a broad range of taxa and ecosystem functions in a non-experimental system, these results are in line with predictions from biodiversity experiments and emphasize that conserving biodiversity is essential for maintaining ecosystem functioning.","author_count":47,"contributors":["Albrecht, Jörg","Peters, Marcell K.","Becker, Joscha N.","Behler, Christina","Classen, Alice","Ensslin, Andreas","Ferger, Stefan W.","Gebert, Friederike","Gerschlauer, Friederike","Helbig-Bonitz, Maria","Kindeketa, William J.","Kühnel, Anna","Mayr, Antonia V.","Njovu, Henry K.","Pabst, Holger","Pommer, Ulf","Röder, Juliane","Rutten, Gemma","Schellenberger Costa, David","Sierra-Cornejo, Natalia","Vogeler, Anna","Vollstädt, Maximilian G. R.","Dulle, Hamadi I.","Eardley, Connal D.","Howell, Kim M.","Keller, Alexander","Peters, Ralph S.","Kakengi, Victor","Hemp, Claudia","Zhang, Jie","Manning, Peter","Mueller, Thomas","Bogner, Christina","Böhning-Gaese, Katrin","Brandl, Roland","Hertel, Dietrich","Huwe, Bernd","Kiese, Ralf","Kleyer, Michael","Leuschner, Christoph","Kuzyakov, Yakov","Nauss, Thomas","Tschapka, Marco","Fischer, Markus","Hemp, Andreas","Steffan-Dewenter, Ingolf","Schleuning, Matthias"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-477298"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Africa","America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Russia","South Africa","Switzerland","Tanzania","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Bavarian State Research Centre for Agriculture","College of African Wildlife Management","German Centre for Integrative Biodiversity Research","Goethe University Frankfurt","Karlsruhe Institute of Technology","Leipzig University","Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich","RUDN University","Senckenberg Biodiversity and Climate Research Institute","Smithsonian Institution","State Office for Geology, Raw Materials and Mining","Swiss Federal Institute for Forest, Snow and Landscape Research","Tanzania Forestry Research Institute","Tanzania Wildlife Research Institute","Ulm University","University of Bayreuth","University of Bern","University of Cologne","University of Dar Es Salaam","University of Göttingen","University of Hamburg","University of KwaZulu-Natal","University of Marburg","University of Oldenburg","University of Würzburg","Zoologisches Forschungsmuseum Alexander Koenig"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"species richness is more important for ecosystem functioning than species turnover along an elevational gradient","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-