","first":"Christoph","key":"leuschnerchristophpau","last":"Leuschner","name":"Leuschner, Christoph","rank":40,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 26","first":"Yakov","key":"kuzyakovyakovpau","last":"Kuzyakov","name":"Kuzyakov, Yakov","rank":41,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"11","first":"Thomas","key":"naussthomaspau","last":"Nauss","name":"Nauss, Thomas","rank":42,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5 27","first":"Marco","key":"tschapkamarcopau","last":"Tschapka","name":"Tschapka, Marco","rank":43,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 6","first":"Markus","key":"fischermarkuspau","last":"Fischer","name":"Fischer, Markus","rank":44,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"24","first":"Andreas","key":"hempandreaspau","last":"Hemp","name":"Hemp, Andreas","rank":45,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Ingolf","key":"steffandewenteringolfpau","last":"Steffan-Dewenter","name":"Steffan-Dewenter, Ingolf","rank":46,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Matthias","key":"schleuningmatthiaspau","last":"Schleuning","name":"Schleuning, Matthias","rank":47,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34545216"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41559-021-01550-9","issn":["2397334X"],"issue":"12","journal":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","pages":"1582-1593","status":"p","title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","title_org":"Nature Ecology and Evolution","volume":"5"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85115248230"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8c3deac5-7caa-4e4e-958b-3bc90c2a1360"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Species richness is more important for ecosystem functioning than species turnover along an elevational gradient","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-477298","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: Inflammation contributes to unstable atherosclerotic plaque and stroke. In randomised trials in patients with coronary disease, canukinumab (an interleukin-1B antagonist) and colchicine (a tubulin inhibitor with pleiotropic anti-inflammatory effects) reduced recurrent vascular events. Hypothesis: Anti-inflammatory therapy with low-dose colchicine plus usual care will reduce recurrent vascular events in patients with non-severe, non-cardioembolic stroke and TIA compared with usual care alone. Design: CONVINCE is a multi-centre international (in 17 countries) Prospective, Randomised Open-label, Blinded-Endpoint assessment (PROBE) controlled Phase 3 clinical trial in 3154 participants. The intervention is colchicine 0.5 mg/day and usual care versus usual care alone (antiplatelet, lipid-lowering, antihypertensive treatment, lifestyle advice). Included patients are at least 40 years, with non-severe ischaemic stroke (modified Rankin score ≤3) or high-risk TIA (ABCD2 > 3, or positive DWI, or cranio-cervical artery stenosis) within 72 hours-28 days of randomisation, with qualifying stroke/TIA most likely caused by large artery stenosis, lacunar disease, or cryptogenic embolism. Exclusions are stroke/TIA caused by cardio-embolism or other defined cause (e.g. dissection), contra-indication to colchicine (including potential drug interactions), or incapacity for participation in a clinical trial. The anticipated median follow-up will be 36 months. The primary analysis will be by intention-to-treat. Outcome: The primary outcome is time to first recurrent ischaemic stroke, myocardial infarction, cardiac arrest, or hospitalisation with unstable angina (non-fatal or fatal). Summary: CONVINCE will provide high-quality randomised data on the efficacy and safety of anti-inflammatory therapy with colchicine for secondary prevention after stroke. Schedule: First-patient first-visit was December 2016. Recruitment to complete in 2021, follow-up to complete in 2023.","author_count":25,"contributors":["Kelly, Peter","Weimar, Christian","Lemmens, Robin","Murphy, Sean","Purroy, Francisco","Arsovska, Anita","Bornstein, Natan M.","Czlonkowska, Anna","Fischer, Urs","Fonseca, Ana Catarina","Forbes, John","Hill, Michael D.","Jatuzis, Dalius","Kõrv, Janika","Kruuse, Christina","Mikulik, Robert","J Nederkoorn, Paul","O’Donnell, Martin","Sandercock, Peter","Tanne, David","Tsivgoulis, Georgios","Walsh, Cathal","Williams, David","Zedde, Marialuisa","Price, Christopher I."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-399544"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","Canada","Czechia","Estonia","Germany","Greece","Ireland","Israel","Italy","Lithuania","Netherlands","North Macedonia","Poland","Spain","Switzerland","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["Academic Medical Centre","All Global Affiliations","Flanders Institute for Biotechnology","IRCCS Azienda Unità Sanitaria Locale di Reggio Emilia","Institute of Psychiatry and Neurology, Warszawa","Masaryk University","National and Kapodistrian University of Athens","Royal College of Surgeons in Ireland","Shaare Zedek Medical Center","University Clinic of Neurology","University College Dublin","University Hospital Bern","University Hospital Essen","University Hospitals Leuven,","University of Bern","University of Calgary","University of Edinburgh","University of Galway","University of Limerick","University of Lleida","University of Newcastle","University of Tartu","Vilnius University"],"keyword":["Colchicine","Inflammation","Ischaemic stroke","Randomised controlled trial"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"colchicine for prevention of vascular inflammation in non cardioemboli